{ "answer": ["Babesiosis","babesia"], "clues": ["Following the American Civil War, northern cattleman noticed that local livestock were dying in droves after coming in contact with healthy appearing cattle from Texas (so called 'Texas Cattle Fever'). Researchers proved that this tiny protozoan parasite was destroying red blood cells and causing the disease. This was the first time that an arthropod was proven to transmit an infectious disease","It's not malaria. The first human case of this was in 1957 where a young cattle farmer fell ill with a high fever, anemia, and ultimately died of kidney failure. He had a prior splenectomy so scientists concluded that this could only affect individuals without a spleen. This was later disproven.","Looking at an infected individual's blood under the microscope, you might see these parasites arranging themselves in a unique tetrad formation that resembles a maltese cross."] },
{ "answer": ["Delirium"], "clues": ["For centuries, this condition was heavily linked to 'putrid fevers' that supposedly boiled the blood and sent toxic vapors to the head. As medicine evolved in the 17th century, scientists blamed it on a disruption of animal spirits","Although Hippocrates used about sixteen different words to refer to and name this clinical syndrome such as 'phrenitis' and 'lethargus', the name we currently use derives from latin meaning 'to go out of the furrow'","Up to half of older adults in the hospital experience this. While previously thought to be temporary and harmless, we now increasingly recognize that affected patients face significantly higher short-term and long-term mortality."] },
